> era5land_australia_tmean_2004.nc4  era5land_australia_tmean_2012.nc4
> era5land_australia_tmean_2005.nc4  era5land_australia_tmean_2013.nc4
> era5land_australia_tmean_2006.nc4  era5land_australia_tmean_2014.nc4
> era5land_australia_tmean_2007.nc4  era5land_australia_tmean_2015.nc4
> era5land_australia_tmean_2008.nc4  era5land_australia_tmean_2016.nc4
> ```
> How do I load all of these in python? I don't want to load them all in memory at once though-- I'll do some operations and then aggregate to the monthly level before writing them back out.
**assistant**:
> Use `xarray` with `dask` and `open_mfdataset` – that will give you a single logical dataset spanning all years, but lazily loaded so nothing big comes into memory until you explicitly compute or write.
> 
> Below is a pattern that should work well for ERA5-style NetCDFs on a cluster like Midway.
> 
> ### 1. Environment setup
> 
> ```bash
> pip install xarray netCDF4 dask[complete] zarr  # or use conda/mamba
> ```
> 
> (or load appropriate modules on Midway, e.g. `module load python xarray dask` depending on your setup.)
> 
> ### 2. Open all yearly files lazily
> 
> ```python
> import xarray as xr
> 
> # Adjust the path pattern as needed
> data_path = "/path/to/tmean/era5land_australia_tmean_*.nc4"
> 
> ds = xr.open_mfdataset(
>     data_path,
>     combine="by_coords",        # typical for ERA5-like files
>     parallel=True,              # use dask for parallel IO/compute
>     chunks={"time": 31}         # or some reasonable time-chunk size
> )
> ```
> 
> Notes:
> 
> - `open_mfdataset` does **not** load all data into memory; it builds a dask-backed dataset.
> - `chunks` controls how data are partitioned. For daily data, e.g. `{"time": 31}` or `{"time": 365}` is often fine; you can add spatial chunks too, e.g. `{"time": 31, "latitude": 200, "longitude": 200}` if the grid is big.
> 
> ### 3. Aggregate to monthly means (still lazy)
> 
> Suppose your temperature variable is named `tmean` (adjust as needed):
> 
> ```python
> # Convert time coordinate to monthly periods and groupby
> ds_monthly = ds.resample(time="1MS").mean()   # 1MS = month start; use "1M" if you prefer month-end
> ```
> 
> This is still lazy: no full array is realized yet.
> 
> If daily data are not exactly regular or you prefer grouping:
> 
> ```python
> ds_monthly = ds.groupby("time.month").mean("time")  # loses year info; usually resample is better
> ```
> 
> For climatology (e.g., mean Jan across all years), you’d use `groupby("time.month")`; for year-month pairs, you could use:
> 
> ```python
> ds_monthly = ds.resample(time="MS").mean()
> ```
> 
> ### 4. Write monthly data back to disk
> 
> Use a chunked, compressed NetCDF with dask:
> 
> ```python
> encoding = {}
> for v in ds_monthly.data_vars:
>     encoding[v] = {
>         "zlib": True,
>         "complevel": 4,
>         "chunksizes": (12, 100, 100) if {"time", "lat", "lon"} <= set(ds_monthly[v].dims) else None
>     }
> 
> out_file = "/path/to/output/era5land_australia_tmean_monthly.nc"
> 
> ds_monthly.to_netcdf(out_file, encoding=encoding, compute=True)
> ```
> 
> - The write triggers the computation, but it is streamed and chunked, so you still don’t hold everything in memory.
> - Adjust `chunksizes` to match your dimensions (e.g. `("time", "latitude", "longitude")`).
> 
> ### 5. If you want one output file per year or per month
> 
> You can loop over years to keep each write small:
> 
> ```python
> import numpy as np
> 
> years = np.unique(ds["time.year"].values)
> 
> for y in years:
>     ds_y = ds.sel(time=str(y))
>     ds_monthly_y = ds_y.resample(time="1MS").mean()
> 
>     out_file = f"/path/to/output/era5land_australia_tmean_monthly_{y}.nc"
>     ds_monthly_y.to_netcdf(out_file)
> ```
> 
> Again, this remains dask-lazy until each `to_netcdf` call.
> 
> ---
> 
> If you tell me:
> 
> - the actual variable name(s),
> - the typical size of each yearly file (e.g. GB, grid dimensions),
> 
> I can suggest more precise chunking choices and encodings tailored for both performance and later analysis.
