> How do I calculate the first-difference (over time) of a xr dataset, for each grid cell?
**assistant**:
> Use `diff` along the time dimension; xarray will do this independently for every grid cell.
> 
> Assuming your dataset is `ds` and the time dimension is called `"time"`:
> 
> ```python
> # First difference along time, for all data variables
> ds_diff = ds.diff(dim="time")
> ```
> 
> That computes, for each variable and grid cell:
> 
> \[
> \text{diff}[t, i, j] = ds[t+1, i, j] - ds[t, i, j]
> \]
> 
> Some useful options:
> 
> - If you only want to difference a particular variable, e.g. `tmean`:
> 
>   ```python
>   da_diff = ds["tmean"].diff("time")
>   ```
> 
> - If your time dimension has a different name, e.g. `"forecast_time"`:
> 
>   ```python
>   ds_diff = ds.diff("forecast_time")
>   ```
> 
> - If you want the difference per unit time (e.g. per day/month) and your time steps aren’t exactly 1 unit apart, you can divide by the time delta:
> 
>   ```python
>   dt = ds["time"].diff("time")  # 1D time intervals
>   # Broadcast dt to all dims of tmean via alignment
>   tmean_per_day = ds["tmean"].diff("time") / dt
>   ```
> 
> If you tell me the variable name and whether you want raw differences or rates (per day/month/year), I can give a more exact snippet.
