> And how do I now subtract that from another variable that has the time time dimension as the original had before I did the FD? I can assume that the FD of the first period was 0.
**assistant**:
> You want to line up a length‑T series with its length‑(T‑1) first difference, assume 0 in the first period, and then subtract from another variable that’s still length‑T.
> 
> Outline:
> 
> 1. Compute first difference (length T‑1).  
> 2. Pad/prepend a zero at the first time step so it becomes length T again.  
> 3. Align time and subtract from the other variable.
> 
> Here’s a concrete pattern.
> 
> ### Example with specific variable names
> 
> Assume:
> 
> - Original dataset: `ds`
> - Time dimension: `"time"`
> - First variable you differenced: `var1`
> - Other variable (same time dim, still full length): `var2`
> 
> ```python
> import xarray as xr
> import numpy as np
> 
> # 1. First difference of var1 (length T-1 along time)
> fd = ds["var1"].diff("time")
> 
> # 2. Pad with an initial 0, assuming FD of first period is 0
> #    - Keep same coords/dims as original
> time = ds["time"]
> 
> # Create a DataArray of zeros for the first time step
> first_zero = xr.DataArray(
>     np.zeros_like(ds["var1"].isel(time=0)),
>     dims=ds["var1"].isel(time=0).dims,
>     coords={dim: ds["var1"].coords[dim] for dim in ds["var1"].isel(time=0).dims}
> )
> 
> # Give it the correct first time coordinate
> first_zero = first_zero.expand_dims(time=[time.isel(time=0)])
> 
> # Now fd has coords for time[1:]; align by concatenating along time
> fd_full = xr.concat([first_zero, fd], dim="time")
> fd_full = fd_full.assign_coords(time=time)  # ensure coordinates match exactly
> 
> # 3. Subtract from var2 (same shape/time as original)
> result = ds["var2"] - fd_full
> ```
> 
> `result` now has one value per original time step, with the first‑difference (0 in the first period) subtracted off for each grid cell.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Simpler option (if shapes are simple)
> 
> If `var1` is just `(time, lat, lon)` and you’re okay with assuming the first value of `var1.diff` is 0 for all lat/lon, you can exploit indexing:
> 
> ```python
> fd = ds["var1"].diff("time")
> 
> # Make an array of zeros matching the first time slice:
> zero_slice = ds["var1"].isel(time=0) * 0
> zero_slice = zero_slice.assign_coords(time=[ds["time"].isel(time=0)])
> 
> fd_full = xr.concat(
>     [zero_slice, fd],
>     dim="time"
> ).assign_coords(time=ds["time"])
> 
> result = ds["var2"] - fd_full
> ```
> 
> If you share the exact shapes/dim names for your variables, I can tighten this to just a few lines tailored to your dataset.
