> Great. So now I have three monthly variables for each region, and the regions are all on the same grid, but different bounding boxes. Can I combine all regions and variables into one file?
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can combine them into a single file. The cleanest way depends on how “regions” are represented in your datasets.
> 
> Let’s assume:
> 
> - Each region is on the **same grid resolution and CRS**, but covers a different spatial subset (bounding box).
> - Each region has monthly variables, e.g. `rainfall_ms`, `swe_ms`, `tmean_ms`, all with the same time axis.
> - You’d like a single global-ish file with:
>   - either one big grid that covers the union of all regions, with NaNs outside each region; or
>   - a single file with a `region` dimension and the original regional bounding boxes.
> 
> I’ll outline both patterns.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Option A: Build a single large grid (union of all regions)
> 
> Use this if all regions lie on a common “global” lat/lon grid (e.g., ERA5) and you want a single spatial raster per time step.
> 
> ### 1. Standardize and “pad” each region to the full grid
> 
> Suppose you’ve already opened monthly datasets like:
> 
> ```python
> ds_a = xr.open_dataset("regionA_monthly.nc")  # has lat, lon subset
> ds_b = xr.open_dataset("regionB_monthly.nc")
> ds_c = xr.open_dataset("regionC_monthly.nc")
> ```
> 
> First, construct a master grid from the union of their coordinates:
> 
> ```python
> import xarray as xr
> 
> # gather all lat/lon coords
> all_lats = xr.concat([ds_a.latitude, ds_b.latitude, ds_c.latitude], dim="tmp_lat").unique()
> all_lons = xr.concat([ds_a.longitude, ds_b.longitude, ds_c.longitude], dim="tmp_lon").unique()
> 
> # sort them
> all_lats = all_lats.sortby(all_lats)
> all_lons = all_lons.sortby(all_lons)
> ```
> 
> Then reindex each dataset to that full grid, which will fill missing cells with NaN:
> 
> ```python
> ds_a_full = ds_a.reindex(latitude=all_lats, longitude=all_lons)
> ds_b_full = ds_b.reindex(latitude=all_lats, longitude=all_lons)
> ds_c_full = ds_c.reindex(latitude=all_lats, longitude=all_lons)
> ```
> 
> If each dataset has a disjoint spatial extent, you can then *merge* variables:
> 
> ```python
> ds_all = xr.merge([ds_a_full, ds_b_full, ds_c_full])
> ```
> 
> or, if each dataset represents different variables for the **same** region set, rename variables first, then merge.
> 
> Finally, write to disk:
> 
> ```python
> ds_all.to_netcdf("all_regions_monthly_uniongrid.nc")
> ```
> 
> Notes:
> 
> - If two regions overlap and define the same variable, you’ll get merge conflicts unless the overlapping values are identical or you specify `compat`/`join` options.
> - This produces one dataset with dimensions `(time, latitude, longitude, ...)` and all variables inside.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Option B: Combine regions via a `region` dimension
> 
> Use this if you want to preserve each region’s bounding box and store them as separate “members” in one file.
> 
> ### 1. Add a `region` coordinate and stack into a new dimension
> 
> Assume each region dataset has the same time axis and variables, but different spatial extents:
> 
> ```python
> regions = {
>     "aus": ds_aus,    # Australia
>     "sea": ds_sea,    # Southeast Asia
>     "sa":  ds_sa,     # South America
> }
> ```
> 
> You can add a region coordinate and then pack them into one dataset using `xr.concat` along a new `region` dimension. Because lat/lon shapes differ, we do this in a slightly more flexible way: store each region as a separate entry in a `region`-indexed Dataset of Datasets is awkward in plain NetCDF; more practical is:
> 
> - Either: Option A (common grid) and then add a `region` mask variable.
> - Or: Use `xarray` with `coords='minimal'` and write to Zarr (supports variable-length axes per region).
> 
> If you **must stay with NetCDF**, the usual approach is:
> 
> ### 2. Create a region mask on the common grid (recommended hybrid)
> 
> Combine Options A and B:
> 
> 1. Build a full union grid as in Option A.
> 2. For each region, build a boolean or integer mask on that grid:
> 
>    ```python
>    import numpy as np
> 
>    mask_aus = xr.full_like(ds_all['some_var'].isel(time=0), False, dtype=bool)
>    # set True where lat/lon fall inside AUS bounding box (or polygon)
>    ```
> 
> 3. Stack these masks into a `region` axis:
> 
>    ```python
>    masks = xr.Dataset({
>        "region_mask": xr.concat(
>            [mask_aus, mask_sea, mask_sa],
>            dim=xr.DataArray(["aus", "sea", "sa"], dims="region", name="region")
>        )
>    })
>    ```
> 
> 4. Merge masks and variables:
> 
>    ```python
>    ds_final = xr.merge([ds_all, masks])
>    ds_final.to_netcdf("all_regions_monthly_with_masks.nc")
>    ```
> 
> Now you have:
> 
> - A single file.
> - One grid.
> - All variables.
> - A `region_mask(region, lat, lon)` variable to identify which part of the grid belongs to which logical region.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Option C: If each region has **all three variables**, just merge per region, then union-grid
> 
> If per-region you have three variables (e.g. `rainfall_ms`, `swe_ms`, `tmean_ms`), first merge within a region:
> 
> ```python
> ds_region = xr.merge([rainfall_ms_region, swe_ms_region, tmean_ms_region])
> ```
> 
> Then apply Option A or hybrid (union grid + region masks) across regions.
